WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.112
In this part we look at the geometric meaning of a first order equation,

2
00:00:05.112 --> 00:00:09.974
solve a Cauchy problem and find out when the solution is unique.

3
00:00:10.224 --> 00:00:18.264
The equation y prime equals f of x and y gives, at each point, the slope of the tangent to a solution graph.

4
00:00:18.264 --> 00:00:22.344
Short segments with these slopes form the direction field.

5
00:00:22.344 --> 00:00:27.278
An integral curve is tangent to the field segment at every point.

6
00:00:27.528 --> 00:00:31.248
To sketch the field by hand we use isoclines.

7
00:00:31.248 --> 00:00:35.424
An isocline is a curve along which the slope is the same.

8
00:00:35.424 --> 00:00:40.502
For this equation the isoclines are circles centred at the origin.

9
00:00:40.752 --> 00:00:47.808
A Cauchy problem: y prime equals two y, with the initial condition y of zero equals three.

10
00:00:47.808 --> 00:00:51.576
The general solution is C e to the two x.

11
00:00:51.576 --> 00:00:56.664
At x equal to zero, y equals C, so C equals three.

12
00:00:56.664 --> 00:01:00.974
Answer: y equals three e to the two x.

13
00:01:01.224 --> 00:01:09.432
Picard’s theorem: if f and its partial derivative with respect to y are continuous in a rectangle around the initial point,

14
00:01:09.432 --> 00:01:13.344
the Cauchy problem has a solution and it is unique.

15
00:01:13.344 --> 00:01:17.750
Note that the guarantee holds only near the initial point.

16
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:20.592
What if the condition fails?

17
00:01:20.592 --> 00:01:26.496
Take y prime equals three y to the two thirds, with y of zero equal to zero.

18
00:01:26.496 --> 00:01:30.480
The derivative with respect to y is infinite at zero.

19
00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:37.392
As a result, both y identically zero and a cubic starting at any later point are solutions.

20
00:01:37.392 --> 00:01:40.046
Uniqueness fails.

21
00:01:40.296 --> 00:01:42.312
Check yourself.

22
00:01:42.312 --> 00:01:46.406
